!% -~S
!% $OMIT_UNUSED_ROUTINES=1

! ============================================================================
!  PUNYINFORM SKELETON  —  Frotzsmith starter (PunyInform)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  A bare, compilable PunyInform starting point. The `!%` lines above are
!  compiler options. Replace the title and author, then add your rooms and
!  objects after puny.h.
!
!  Every other line that starts with `!` is a COMMENT: the compiler skips
!  from `!` to the end of the line. This file is meant to be read top to
!  bottom; each piece of code is explained just above it, and the notes say
!  where PunyInform differs from the Standard Library (std-skeleton.inf is
!  the same game written for the Standard Library — read them side by side).
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  THE `!%` COMPILER OPTION LINES (top of file — keep them first)
!  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
!  Lines that begin with `!%` at the very top of the main source file are
!  read by the Inform 6 compiler itself BEFORE compilation starts, as if
!  they had been typed on its command line. Further down the file, a `!%`
!  line would just be an ordinary comment, so they must stay at the top.
!
!    !% -~S          turns OFF strict mode. Strict mode (on unless you
!                    switch it off) adds run-time checks that catch mistakes
!                    such as reading a property of something that is not an
!                    object; the price is a larger, slower story file.
!                    PunyInform is all about small and fast, so its own game
!                    template switches strict mode off. It is a
!                    recommendation, not a requirement: this file compiles
!                    and plays without the line too.
!    !% $OMIT_UNUSED_ROUTINES=1
!                    tells the compiler to strip routines that are never called,
!                    keeping the output file as small as possible.
!
!  KEY CONCEPTS IN THIS FILE
!  ─────────────────────────
!  • Constant                  — compile-time name/value pair
!  • INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE    — PunyInform-specific constant; tells the
!                                library where to place the player at startup
!                                (replaces `location = …` in Initialise)
!  • Include "globals.h"       — PunyInform global variables & configuration
!  • Include "puny.h"          — the full PunyInform library (parser + verbs +
!                                grammar all in one; no separate Grammar include)
!  • Object … has light        — a room needs the `light` attribute so the
!                                player can see inside it
!  • [ Initialise; ]           — the startup routine. It must EXIST (the
!                                library calls it; leave it out and the
!                                compiler stops with "No such constant as
!                                Initialise"), but in PunyInform it need not
!                                set the location, so here it only prints a
!                                welcome line
!
!  PUNYINFORM vs STANDARD LIBRARY
!  ────────────────────────────────
!  Standard Library:           PunyInform equivalent:
!    Include "Parser";   ──►   (bundled inside puny.h)
!    Include "VerbLib";  ──►   (bundled inside puny.h)
!    location = X in           INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE = X  (constant)
!      Initialise;
!    Include "Grammar";  ──►   (bundled inside puny.h — no separate include)
!    Include "Exits";    ──►   (none: Frotzsmith's Exits.h is Standard-
!                               Library-only, so rooms here print no
!                               "Obvious exits:" line)
!
!  PunyInform is designed so games fit and run well even in the oldest,
!  smallest Z-machine format, z3. Frotzsmith compiles the PunyInform samples
!  as z5 unless a sample picks another format (the Haunted House port picks
!  z3). The Standard Library needs z5 or larger.
!
!  HOW THE GAME FLOWS
!    One room, the Starting Room, and nothing in it yet. LOOK shows it again;
!    everything else gets the library's stock replies. Copy this file to begin
!    a game of your own.
!
!  WHAT THE PLAYER SEES AT START, IN ORDER
!    1. "Your story begins here..."  (printed by Initialise)
!    2. The banner: Story, Headline, release/serial/library line
!    3. The first room description (the library moves the player into
!       the starting room, which describes it)
! ============================================================================

! --- Game identity constants -------------------------------------------------
!  `Constant` declares a name for a value fixed at compile time.
!  `Story` is the title and `Headline` the subtitle; PunyInform's banner
!  prints both when the game starts. (The status bar at the top shows the
!  current room and the score/moves, not the title.)
!  The `^` escape character inside strings produces a newline when printed.

Constant Story "MY GAME";
Constant Headline "^An interactive fiction by Anonymous.^";

! --- Starting location ------------------------------------------------------
!  INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE is a PunyInform convention. globals.h reads this
!  constant and uses it as the starting value of the global `location`
!  (`Global location = INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE;`), and after Initialise the
!  library moves the player there. So it must be defined BEFORE
!  Include "globals.h".
!  You do NOT need to set `location` manually inside [ Initialise; ] when
!  using PunyInform — this constant handles it automatically. (Setting it in
!  Initialise works too. Leave both out and the player starts in darkness.)
!  `StartRoom` is only declared further down; naming an object before its
!  declaration is allowed.
!  The `=` is optional: `Constant INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE StartRoom;` is the
!  same.

Constant INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE = StartRoom;

! --- PunyInform library includes --------------------------------------------
!  Include "globals.h" first. It declares global variables (location, score,
!  turns, etc.), the attributes (light, static ...) and properties
!  (description, n_to ...) your objects use, and reads your configuration
!  constants (like INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE, or the OPTIONAL_... switches that
!  turn on extra library features). It must come before puny.h.

Include "globals.h";

!  Include "puny.h" is the entire PunyInform library in one file. It contains
!  the parser, all built-in verbs, and the grammar tables. Unlike the Standard
!  Library there is no separate "Grammar" include at the end of the file.
!  Put your Object, Class and Verb definitions after this include, as every
!  PunyInform sample does. (`Extend`, which adds to one of the library's
!  verbs, has to come after it.)

Include "puny.h";

! --- Rooms and objects -------------------------------------------------------
!  Define your starting room. The word immediately after `Object` is the
!  internal identifier; the quoted string is the short name shown to the player.
!  The syntax is the same as in the Standard Library:
!    Object identifier "short name"
!      with property value, property value,   ! PROPERTIES: named values
!      has  attribute attribute;              ! ATTRIBUTES: on/off flags
!
!  `with description "…"` provides the text printed when the player LOOKs.
!
!  `has light` marks the room as lit. A room without `light` is pitch-dark;
!  PunyInform shows "Darkness / It is pitch dark here!" until a
!  carried light source (also given the `light` attribute) is present.
!  (The Standard Library's wording is "It is pitch dark, and you can't see a
!  thing." — same idea, different message text.)

Object StartRoom "Starting Room"
  with description "An empty room, waiting for your imagination.",
  has  light;

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Add your rooms and objects here.
!
!  ROOM CONNECTIONS
!  Attach directional properties to room objects to link them:
!    n_to NextRoom,    ← NORTH leads to NextRoom
!    s_to PrevRoom,    ← SOUTH leads to PrevRoom
!  Full list: n_to s_to e_to w_to ne_to nw_to se_to sw_to u_to d_to
!             in_to out_to
!  Links are one-way; give the other room the matching property too.
!
!  PLACING OBJECTS IN ROOMS (arrow / indent notation)
!  An `Object ->` (one arrow) places the object inside the preceding room or
!  container at compile time:
!    Object -> key "brass key"
!      with name 'brass' 'key',
!           description "A small brass key.";
!  `name` lists the words (single quotes) the player can type for it.
!
!  Two arrows `Object -> ->` nest the object inside the previous child object.
!
!  COMMON ATTRIBUTES
!  has light      — room or portable lamp is lit
!  has static     — cannot be taken ("That's fixed in place.")
!  has scenery    — can't be taken and omitted from the room's object listing
!  has container  — can hold objects; add `open` to start open
!  has supporter  — objects can be placed ON it
!  has openable   — player can OPEN/CLOSE it
!  has open       — starts open (open is not the same as unlocked: see
!                   `locked` / `lockable` in the Locked Chest sample)
!  has concealed  — present but not listed in room descriptions
!
!  A PUNYINFORM DETAIL WORTH KNOWING EARLY
!  To save memory, PunyInform lets some properties share storage ("alias"):
!  for example `with_key` shares with e_to and `door_to` with n_to. Rooms
!  use the direction properties and things use the others, so normally you
!  never notice — but a thing that needs both (an object with an e_to AND a
!  with_key) would clash. The Standard Library keeps them all separate.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

! --- Initialise routine -----------------------------------------------------
!  A routine is written `[ Name; statements ];`. PunyInform calls
!  [ Initialise; ] once, before the banner and before the player is moved
!  into the starting room. It is required (see the header), but in
!  PunyInform it is optional for setting location (the constant above
!  handles that). Use it to print a welcome line or run any other one-time
!  setup code before the first prompt.
!
!  `print "…^"` prints the string followed by a newline (`^` = newline).
!  `print "…^^"` prints the string then TWO newlines (a blank line gap).
!
!  The return value matters in both libraries: return 2 and the banner is
!  skipped. This routine just ends at `];`, which returns 0, so the banner
!  follows the message. (A bare string, `"^Your story begins here...^";`,
!  would print the same text and return true; that works in PunyInform too.)

[ Initialise;
    print "^Your story begins here...^";
];
